# Reasonable Compensation

Reasonable compensation is the requirement for S Corporation shareholder-employees to receive market-rate salary for their work, in addition to any distributions. This requirement prevents owners from paying themselves minimal salary (to reduce self-employment tax) and taking the remainder as distributions.

## Why It Matters
- S Corps allow avoiding self-employment tax on distributions (only salary is subject to payroll tax)
- Without the reasonable compensation requirement, owners could pay themselves $1 salary and take large distributions tax-free
- The IRS requires S Corp owners who perform services to receive "reasonable" compensation

## Determining Reasonable Compensation
Factors include:
- Role and responsibilities
- Industry standards and comparable salaries
- Business revenue and profitability
- Owner's qualifications and experience
- Time commitment to the business

## Tax Implications
- Salary is subject to payroll taxes (Social Security, Medicare) but deductible as business expense
- Distributions are not subject to self-employment tax but taxed as ordinary income
- Optimizing the salary-to-distribution ratio is a key S Corp tax strategy

